# Tenant migrations

One EF context: `AppDbContext` (`{Product}.Infrastructure/Data/Migrations`). **One database per tenant** (`TenantSettings:Tenants`). Each tenant DB has its own `dbo.__EFMigrationsHistory`; applying on local does **not** update other tenants.

## Add a migration (design-time → local tenant only)

`dotnet ef` uses `AppDbContextFactory` / `DesignTimeConfiguration`, which targets `TenantSettings:Tenants:local` (fallback: first tenant). Scaffolding only.

```bash
dotnet ef migrations add <Name> \
  --project backend/{Product}.Infrastructure \
  --startup-project backend/{Product}.API \
  --context AppDbContext \
  --output-dir Data/Migrations
```

Do not use `dotnet ef database update` for anything but local.

## Apply migrations (all tenants)

```bash
dotnet run --project backend/{Product}.Migrator                         # all enabled tenants + seed
dotnet run --project backend/{Product}.Migrator -- --tenant local       # one tenant
dotnet run --project backend/{Product}.Migrator -- --tenant a --tenant b --no-seed
powershell -File backend/scripts/Update-AllTenantDatabases.ps1
```

Or set `Database:MigrateOnStartup: true` — the API migrates every enabled tenant at boot (failures are logged per tenant, API still starts).

## Agent checklist

- After adding a migration, remind the user to run the Migrator (or restart with `MigrateOnStartup`) so **all** tenants update.
- Follow `dbo-schema-for-new-tables.mdc`; verify `schema: "dbo"` in the scaffolded file.
- Data migrations (`migrationBuilder.Sql`) run per tenant — make them idempotent (`IF NOT EXISTS`).
- Permission seed rows live in `PermissionCatalog` (EF `HasData`); never renumber existing IDs.
- Never put real connection strings or passwords in rules or commits.
